  "role": "You are the pediatric hematology-oncology fellow caring for Jordan Williams, a thirteen-year-old with HbSS sickle cell disease whose hydroxyurea adherence has been inconsistent.",
  "summary": "Monique Williams is the mother and primary caregiver of Jordan, thirteen, who has HbSS sickle cell disease.",
  "scenario": "Monique Williams is the mother and primary caregiver of Jordan, thirteen, who has HbSS sickle cell disease. He has recurrent vaso-occlusive crises, has missed several hematology appointments, and takes hydroxyurea inconsistently.\n\nYou are meeting her one-on-one in an outpatient hematology clinic room to understand what is actually getting in the way and to build a plan with her. Jordan is at school; she has come from a work shift.\n\nHer objection is not unreasonable. Hydroxyurea makes Jordan nauseated and tired, she has watched it happen, and nobody has given her a straight answer about whether that trade is worth it. Underneath the objection is something older: she and Jordan have been treated as drug-seeking during pain crises.\n\nWhat makes this hard is that you have to be accurate about a drug whose evidence is strong in some places and thin in others, name a pattern you were not present for without adjudicating who did what, and do both without retreating from a clear recommendation. Honest about the evidence, clear about the advice. Either half without the other fails the encounter.",
