        "rubricName": "Pediatric Hematology-Oncology — ICS1: Patient- and Family-Centered Communication",
        "content": "## ICS1: Patient- and Family-Centered Communication — Level 4 / 5 · Proficient\nPediatric Hematology-Oncology milestone · levels 1–5, novice → expert (Dreyfus)\n\n**Assigned level:** 4 · Proficient — the highest level fully demonstrated. The learner navigated an encounter opening with explicit mistrust and racial injustice, repaired the relationship, and built a personalized shared plan.\n\n**Level progression:** Levels 1–4 demonstrated; Level 5 not demonstrated (no opportunity — no learner or trainee was present to mentor or coach).\n\n## Evidence by level\n\n### Level 1 · Novice — Demonstrated\nShowed respect and attempted rapport from the first, hostile turn, and tried to adjust to the parent's stated expectation of directness.\n\"I hear the frustration in your voice, and I'm, I just wanna say I'm really sorry.\"\n\"No, I would love to be direct with you and, and really hear about what's been going on and how we can be doing better.\"\n\n### Level 2 · Advanced beginner — Demonstrated\nBuilt a therapeutic relationship and actively worked to mitigate barriers — the parent's mistrust, fragmented information sources, and time constraints. Assessed the parent's baseline understanding before adding to it.\n\"So tell me a little bit about what you know about hydroxyurea.\"\n\"one of the things I'm wondering is... w- where are you gathering your information?\"\n\n### Level 3 · Competent — Demonstrated\nEstablished a culturally aware, therapeutic relationship in a genuinely difficult encounter opened by an accusation about how Black patients are treated. Communicated with compassion, elicited the family's values and lived reality, and — most notably — acknowledged uncertainty honestly rather than papering over it.\n\"your question about safety... long term, I can't answer directly for you because generally, for a long time, kids who had sickle cell disease didn't live a long time, and so we don't have that data.\"\n\"really get a better understanding about what life looks like for you so we can support Jordan as best as possible.\"\n\n### Level 4 · Proficient — Demonstrated\nSustained a therapeutic relationship through an encounter loaded with ambiguity (unknown long-term safety) and conflict/mistrust, then used genuine shared decision making to build a personalized plan — including determining how and when to bring the adolescent patient into the decision.\n\"is it okay if we sit for a second and try to think through... some things that might make it easier for both you and Jordan to take it?\"\n\"the way that I would end up proposing it to Jordan is twofold. One... take it at nighttime so that when his peak effects... happen, they happen when he's sleeping... The alternative would be to try to take it every morning and take it with food...\"\n\"is there anything else you want me to specifically address with Jordan when I meet him?\"\n\n### Level 5 · Expert — Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\nLevel 5 requires mentoring, modeling, and coaching *others* in family-centered communication. No learner, student, or junior trainee was present in this dyadic encounter, so the scenario created no occasion to demonstrate teaching or coaching. This absence does not count against the learner.\n\n## Rationale\nThe learner met every assessable behavior through Level 4. They entered a charged encounter — an opening accusation of systemic racism and a demand for honesty — and did not deflect; they invited directness and delivered it. They assessed the parent's existing understanding, named where information had been fragmented, and gave a plain-language account of what hydroxyurea does. Critically, when pressed on long-term safety, the learner resisted the temptation to reassure falsely and instead named the limits of the evidence in a way the parent explicitly recognized as trustworthy (\"That's honest. I appreciate that. Nobody's told me that before\"). This honest handling of uncertainty is the fulcrum of Level 3. The learner then elicited the family's concrete circumstances (two jobs, an adolescent who hides pills, nausea as the deal-breaker) and co-constructed a plan tailored to those realities — a nighttime dosing option chosen because the parent could witness it and the adolescent could sleep through side effects — while deliberately reserving the adolescent's own voice for the next visit. That is shared decision making producing a personalized care plan in a complex, initially adversarial encounter: Level 4.\n\n## Why not the next level\nLevel 5 is defined by mentoring, modeling, and coaching *others* to build therapeutic relationships and communicate in a family-centered way. The encounter was a single clinician–parent dyad with no learner present, so there was no opportunity to observe teaching or coaching behaviors. The assessment holds at Level 4 because the simulation did not surface a Level 5 occasion (no opportunity), not because the learner fell short.\n\n## Recommendations\n- To keep strengthening Level 4 work, tighten the shared-decision framing so the *parent* generates options too, not only reacts to yours — e.g., \"Before I offer ideas, what have you noticed helps his nausea, or what have you already tried?\" This makes the plan even more co-owned.\n- A small precision point: the earlier reassurance \"it wouldn't be a recommendation if it weren't safe\" was appropriately caught by the parent as circular, and you recovered well by naming the evidence gap. Lead with that honest framing next time rather than reaching for it only under challenge.\n- For Level 5, seek encounters where you can be observed and can teach — precept a student through a mistrust-laden visit like this one, then debrief how you repaired rapport and handled uncertainty, so your skill becomes transferable coaching.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths** — Turned an openly hostile, mistrust-driven opening into a collaborative alliance. Elicited values and lived circumstances, handled scientific uncertainty with rare honesty that visibly rebuilt trust, and constructed a personalized, feasible plan that reserved space for the adolescent's own voice.\n- **Priority gaps** — No true clinical gap surfaced at the assessed levels; the only unmet behaviors (Level 5 mentoring/coaching) had no opportunity to appear in a dyadic encounter. The one weak moment — the circular safety reassurance — was self-corrected.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Elicit family-generated options before offering your own, deepening shared ownership of the plan.\n  2. Lead with honest framing of evidence limits proactively, rather than only when challenged.\n  3. Pursue observed, teaching-oriented encounters to develop and demonstrate Level 5 coaching behaviors.",
