        "gradeId": "1c3c7842-36bd-4232-b881-cba45fc8735e",
        "rubricName": "Pediatric Hematology-Oncology — PROF1: Professional Behavior",
        "content": "## PROF1: Professional Behavior — Level 4 / 5 · Proficient\nPediatric Hematology-Oncology milestone · levels 1–5, novice → expert (Dreyfus)\n\n**Assigned level:** 4 · Proficient — the highest level fully demonstrated on assessable behaviors; Level 5's community/specialty engagement is not assessable in this single encounter.\n\n**Level progression:** Levels 1–4 demonstrated; Level 5 partially demonstrated (modeling of professional behavior shown; coaching others and extending role beyond patient care not assessable here).\n\n## Evidence by level\n\n### Level 1 · Novice — Demonstrated\nIdentifies expected professional behaviors and potential triggers for lapses, and shows the vocational value of the role. The learner opened a charged, distrust-laden encounter by naming the emotion and expected professional stance: \"I hear the frustration in your voice, and I'm, I just wanna say I'm really sorry.\" The learner clearly recognized the racism/distrust trigger for a professionalism lapse and stayed centered rather than defensive.\n\n### Level 2 · Advanced beginner — Demonstrated\nDemonstrates professional behavior with accountability for patient care. The learner took ownership of the therapeutic relationship and committed to a direct standard: \"No, I would love to be direct with you and, and really hear about what's been going on and how we can be doing better.\" This accepts institutional accountability without deflecting.\n\n### Level 3 · Competent — Demonstrated\nMaintains professional behavior in an increasingly complex and stressful situation and fully engages in care. The opening turn accused the profession of not listening to Black patients — a high-stakes, adversarial start. The learner never became defensive, stayed engaged, and held professional composure throughout: \"So tell me a little bit about what you know about hydroxyurea,\" and pressed forward to a concrete plan rather than retreating.\n\n### Level 4 · Proficient — Demonstrated\nRecognizes situations that may trigger professionalism lapses and intervenes to prevent them, and exhibits a sense of duty. The learner recognized that a \"pretty answer\" would erode trust and instead met the parent's explicit demand for honesty — including admitting the limits of the evidence: \"your question about safety, uh, long term, I can't answer directly for you because... for a long time, kids who had sickle cell disease didn't live a long time, and so we don't have that data.\" This is proactive integrity — refusing false reassurance that would have been a professionalism lapse in the moment. The learner also demonstrated duty by orienting the whole plan around the patient's lived experience and autonomy: \"I really am curious what it feels like to be in Jordan's body,\" and building a feasible, shared plan.\n\n### Level 5 · Expert — Partially demonstrated / Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\nThe learner modeled exemplary professional behavior (honesty, humility, respect). However, Level 5 also requires coaching others whose behavior fails professional expectations and extending the role beyond patient care into community, specialty, and profession. No other clinician or trainee appeared for the learner to coach, and the encounter offered no occasion to demonstrate community/specialty engagement — so these defining behaviors could not be surfaced here.\n\n## Rationale\nThe learner met every assessable behavior through Level 4. Facing an opening accusation of systemic racism and a parent primed for a \"dance around\" evasion, the learner sustained composure, engaged fully, and — critically — recognized the high-risk moment where a reassuring but dishonest answer would have been the professionalism lapse. Choosing candor about the evidence gap (\"we just don't know yet\") is exactly the proactive lapse-prevention and sense of duty that defines Level 4. The learner also converted trust into a concrete, patient-centered plan honoring the adolescent's autonomy.\n\n## Why not the next level\nLevel 5 turns on coaching others when their behavior fails professional expectations and extending the pediatric hematologist/oncologist's role into the community, specialty, and profession. Neither was surfaceable in a single dyadic parent encounter — no colleague or trainee was present to coach, and the scenario created no occasion for community/specialty engagement. The assessment holds at Level 4 because the simulation could not surface these behaviors (no opportunity), not because the learner fell short.\n\n## Recommendations\n- To reach Level 5, seek encounters with colleagues or trainees where you can model and, when needed, coach professional behavior — e.g., debriefing a team member who dismissed a distrustful family, naming the pattern and modeling the repair.\n- Continue the candor that worked here; one strengthening move would be to close the loop on your own honesty commitment by naming a concrete accountability step, e.g., \"I'll write up exactly what we don't know about long-term safety so every clinician who sees Jordan gives you the same straight answer I did — not another version pieced together.\"\n- Keep pairing honesty with autonomy-centered planning; it is the core of what earned the parent's trust.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths** — Under a directly adversarial, distrust-laden opening, the learner stayed composed, refused defensiveness, and — most impressively — met the parent's demand for honesty by admitting the limits of the evidence rather than offering false reassurance. That integrity, plus a patient-centered plan honoring the adolescent's autonomy, rebuilt trust (\"This was... this was different\").\n- **Priority gaps** — The learner made two factual missteps (\"it wouldn't be a recommendation if it weren't safe\") before self-correcting toward candor; guard against reflexive reassurance even briefly. The two Level 5 behaviors (coaching others; extending role beyond patient care) were not assessable in this dyadic format.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. In future high-distrust encounters, lead with epistemic honesty from the first factual question rather than after being pushed twice.\n  2. Seek team-based settings to practice modeling and coaching professionalism in others (Level 5).\n  3. Keep anchoring plans in the patient's and family's lived reality and autonomy — this was the encounter's strongest feature.",
